WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.840
It’s crazy when you think about all of
the different kinds of foods we eat.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.760
We just swallow and hope
it all works out for the best.

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:11.280
Well, as it turns out,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:15.680
there are better ways to think about keeping
our bodies humming healthfully along.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.080
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:21.200
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:26.000
Today, we look at how a plant-
based lifestyle is put to the test

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:29.360
against early and late-stage cancer.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:31.840
Higher consumption of
a healthy plant-based diet

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:34.240
was significantly associated
with a lower chance

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.320
of having an elevated PSA,

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.560
which can be a sign
of prostate cancer.

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.480
We're talking about
potentially cutting the odds

00:00:40.480 --> 00:00:44.000
of having an elevated PSA
in half with diet.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.160
The researchers concluded:
"This study provides

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:48.080
strong evidence
supporting the potential use

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:49.840
of plant-based foods
in the prevention

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:52.560
and treatment
of prostate cancer."

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.600
"Not so fast", replied
an editor of the journal,

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:58.400
"This paper certainly
doesn't present final,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.480
convincing proof
that reduced PSA levels

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.960
due to a plant-based diet
in fact translate

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.560
into what we actually care
about: less prostate cancer."

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:09.120
Harvard cohort researchers
took up the mantle

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:10.320
to answer that question.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:11.760
And indeed,
greater consumption

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.400
of a healthful
plant-based diet does appear

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.560
to reduce the risk
of getting prostate cancer

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:18.560
and dying from prostate cancer

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:22.160
by nearly half
for men under age 65.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.520
And those adhering more to a healthy
plant-based diet who do get cancer

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:26.800
had 90 percent lower odds

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.000
of ending up with high-grade,
more severe prostate cancer

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.960
and appeared to be better able
to handle cancer treatment

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.600
and ended up with
better sexual function,

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:38.800
less incontinence,
and more vitality.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.040
This is consistent with the role
of diet and nutrition

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:43.360
in the prevention and treatment
of cancer more generally,

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:45.120
where the evidence points
to a diet

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.520
with minimal animal products.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.040
What if you already
have cancer though?

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:53.840
Study findings suggest that
adhering to plant-based diets

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.640
may be beneficial
for overall cancer survival

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:59.440
and may potentially improve
the prognosis for common cancers

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:03.040
such as breast, colorectal,
and prostate cancers.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.880
It starts with
case reports like this.

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.080
A 73-year-old man
with prostate cancer

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:09.440
was placed
on a plant-based diet,

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.120
and within 10 days,

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.960
his tumor markers were down
about 75 percent.

00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:18.240
But case reports
are like glorified anecdotes.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.160
You can't know
if a healthier diet

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:21.520
and lifestyle actually help

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:24.320
until you run a randomized,
controlled trial

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.200
and put it to the test.

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.920
Dr. Dean Ornish,
famous for showing

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.960
the progression of heart disease
could be reversed,

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:34.480
opening up arteries
without drugs,

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.400
without surgery,
just a plant-based diet

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.840
and lifestyle program,
eliminating angina chest pain

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.680
in about three-quarters
of patients

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:43.440
within a matter of months.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.720
He decided to move
from killer #1— heart disease—

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:48.880
to killer #2—cancer.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:53.200
93 men with early-stage
watch-and-wait prostate cancer

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:54.960
were randomized
to either standard care

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:59.760
or a plant-based diet along
with walking and relaxation.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.560
The cancer tended to get worse
in the control group

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.840
but comparatively better
in the plant-based group.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:09.040
When blood from the patients
on the standard diet

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.720
was dripped onto prostate cancer
cells growing in a petri dish,

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:16.560
cancer cell growth rates
were cut by 9 percent.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.400
Their bodies were doing the best
they could to beat back the cancer.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:23.920
The blood of patients on the
plant-based program, though,

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:27.680
knocked down the cancer
growth by 70 percent.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:29.200
Eating and living
more healthfully

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:33.680
made their blood stream almost eight
times less hospitable to cancer.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.680
Now, this was after a year.

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:37.440
Subsequent studies
have shown that you can see

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.600
a significant
cancer-fighting effect

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.520
after less than two weeks

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:45.760
on a similar Pritikin-style
plant-based diet with exercise.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.560
Ornish took biopsies from
the prostate cancer patients

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.760
before and after three months
and saw a significant change

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.950
in the expression
of more than 500 genes.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.800
Of course,
what we really care about

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:58.480
are disease outcomes.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.120
By the two-year follow-up,
significantly more men

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:04.080
in the control group
had enough cancer progression

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:06.640
that they had to go
into treatment, for example,

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:09.200
five had to get
radical prostatectomies,

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:11.440
which can lead
to urinary incontinence

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:14.080
and impotence in 60 percent,

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:16.640
whereas none of the men
in the lifestyle group

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:18.880
were forced to go into surgery.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:22.160
What about those
with more serious disease?

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.920
Researchers followed 10 patients
with recurrent prostate cancer,

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:28.960
meaning those whose
invasive cancer had to be cut

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:30.560
or burned out with radiation,

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:33.520
yet their PSA levels
started to rise again,

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:36.240
signaling the cancer was back.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.880
There was no control group
per se in this study.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:41.040
Each patient was
kind of their own control

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:43.040
before and after,
being asked to center

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:45.840
their diets around
more whole plant foods.

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:49.440
There was a significant decrease
in the rate of PSA rise,

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:52.400
suggesting a slowing
of cancer growth.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.320
4 out of 10 patients

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.480
actually saw their PSA levels
go down,

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:57.600
suggesting the possibility

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.120
of at least some degree
of disease remission.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:05.280
9 out of 10 had a reduction
in their rates of PSA rise

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.720
as well as an improvement
of their PSA doubling times,

00:05:08.720 --> 00:05:13.680
which is a proxy for how fast
the tumor doubles in size.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.000
The increase
in average doubling time

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:19.040
went from about 12 months
before the dietary change

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:21.920
to 112 months,

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.040
which is the largest that has
ever been reported in any kind

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:28.880
of dietary intervention trial
for recurrent prostate cancer.

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:31.040
What about
really advanced cancer,

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.600
stage four with spread
to distant organs?

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.280
Out of nine patients
willing to go on

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.520
a plant-based
macrobiotic diet,

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.080
three men experienced
long-term regression

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.240
of multiple
bone lesions,

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:44.080
compared to no regression
or healing

00:05:44.080 --> 00:05:46.000
in nine matched
control subjects

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:49.120
who continued eating
the standard American diet.

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:50.720
Those on the healthier diet

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:53.840
went on to live
an average of 19 years more,

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.000
compared to those with
the same severity of cancer

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.520
not even living
for 4 years more, on average,

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:01.120
on their standard diet.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.920
Now this was not
a randomized study,

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:05.360
so maybe it says something

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:07.600
about those
willing to eat healthfully

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:09.920
rather than
healthful eating itself.

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:12.000
But what's the downside?

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:13.520
As Dr. Ornish likes to say,

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:16.960
"The only side effects
are good ones."

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.160
A large number of patients

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.480
with metastatic prostate cancer,
for example,

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:22.960
die not from their cancer,

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:25.360
but rather
from non-cancer causes,

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.760
and the number one cause
is heart disease,

00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:34.445
so, a heart-healthy diet
could only help.

00:06:34.445 --> 00:06:38.560
Dr. Dean Ornish showed that a plant-
based diet and lifestyle program

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:42.320
could apparently reverse the
progression of prostate cancer

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:48.560
by making men’s bloodstream nearly 8 times
better at suppressing cancer cell growth,

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:53.440
but this was for early stage, localized,
watch-and-wait prostate cancer.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.320
What about for more advanced
stage life-threatening disease?

00:06:58.320 --> 00:07:02.400
There had been sporadic case reports
in the literature suggestive of benefit.

00:07:02.400 --> 00:07:06.560
A man, for example, with
extensive metastatic disease,

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:11.120
given maybe three years to live,
goes on a strict plant-based diet.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:15.040
Four years later, it appears
the cancer has disappeared.

00:07:15.040 --> 00:07:19.440
Six years in, he gets a little cocky
and backslides a little bit on the diet.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.720
Cancer comes raging
back and he dies.

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.720
But, that could have been
a total coincidence.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:31.280
That’s the problem with case reports,
which are just kind of glorified anecdotes—

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:37.440
you have no idea how representative the
outcome is unless it’s formally studied.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:44.640
But throughout the 20th century, all we had
were these kinds of case reports... until 2001.

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:48.160
So, we had this preliminary evidence
based on all the case reports

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.800
that prostate cancer may be sensitive
to diet even after it metastasizes,

00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.360
may prolong survival
and even cause remission

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:58.960
of bone metastases in men
with advanced disease.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:04.720
So, researchers decided to put it to
the test, 4-month intervention.

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:09.120
They figured too much saturated fat,
too little fiber, and too much meat

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.880
may be the biggest players in
tumor promotion and progression.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:15.840
So, they put people on a
whole food plant-based diet

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:20.000
of whole grains, beans,
seeds, and fruit.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:24.880
Figuring this would be quite the
departure from their regular diet,

00:08:24.880 --> 00:08:30.800
they included a stress reduction component
in hopes of improving dietary compliance.

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:33.600
Okay so, who were
these ten men?

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.680
They all didn’t just
have prostate cancer;

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:41.120
they all had underwent a radical
prostatectomy to remove their primary tumor,

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:45.520
and then subsequently
had increasing PSA levels,

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.280
indicative of probable
metastatic disease.

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:54.800
PSA stands for prostate-specific antigen;
it’s only made by prostate cells,

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:57.920
and they just had their
entire prostates removed.

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.400
So the level
should be zero.

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.840
The fact that they not
only still had some PSA,

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:07.040
but that it was rising suggests
that the surgery failed,

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:11.134
and the cancer had spread
and was making a comeback.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:17.280
The cancer would presumably still be powering
away and spreading just as fast as before.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:20.800
Instead, this happened.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:24.720
In two men, it looks like the cancer
accelerated, grew even faster,

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:27.920
but in the other 8 men, the
intervention appeared to work,

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:30.800
apparently slowing
down cancer growth,

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.120
and in three it didn’t
just slow or stop,

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:37.520
but appeared to
reverse and shrink.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:39.920
Why the different
responses?

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:43.120
Well, in the Ornish study,
the more people complied

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:47.200
with the diet and lifestyle
recommendations, the better they did.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:52.080
Dietary changes only work
if you actually do them.

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:55.440
Just because you tell people to start
eating a whole food plant-based diet,

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:58.320
doesn’t mean patients
actually do it.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:03.440
One can use fiber intake as a
proxy for dietary compliance,

00:10:03.440 --> 00:10:05.760
since all whole plant
foods have fiber,

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:11.680
and Ornish’s patients about doubled
their fiber intake from 31 to 59.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:13.520
How did this group do?

00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:18.000
They started out even worse,
averaging 14 grams a day,

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:21.360
and only made it up
to 19 grams a day.

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:24.240
That’s not a whole
food plant-based diet—

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.720
that doesn’t even make it up to
the recommended minimum daily intake.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:34.080
If you look closely, only 4 men
increased their fiber intake at all.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:37.360
So maybe that may explain
the different responses.

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:39.920
Like, how did
patient 2 do?

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:44.480
The man whose fiber improved the
most had the best PSA result,

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:48.880
and the man whose fiber intake dropped
the most had the worst PSA result.

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:51.200
And indeed, it appears the
more change they made

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:55.360
to their diet, the
better their results.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.320
The researchers concluded that
a plant-based diet delivered

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:04.320
in the context of stress management
may slow the rate of tumor progression,

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:08.960
and unlike other treatments, may give
patients some control over their disease.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:11.200
And, as Ornish
pointed out,

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:15.760
“the only side effects
are beneficial ones.”

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:17.600
Finally today,

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:21.200
we look at what happens when
metastatic prostate cancer patients

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:25.600
were taught to increase intake of
whole grains, vegetables, fruits, and beans,

00:11:25.600 --> 00:11:30.320
and to decrease consumption
of meat, dairy, and junk.

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:33.680
Dr. Ornish and colleagues were
able to show an apparent reversal

00:11:33.680 --> 00:11:36.640
in the progression of early
stage localized prostate cancer

00:11:36.640 --> 00:11:39.680
with a plant-based diet
and lifestyle program,

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:43.760
and researchers at the University of Massachusetts
and elsewhere showed a similar diet

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:49.360
may help slow the progression of even advanced
prostate cancer over a period of four months.

00:11:49.360 --> 00:11:51.360
How about six months?

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:55.200
Researchers at UC San Diego found more
cancer patients in the same situation

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:57.440
and put them through
the same protocol.

00:11:57.440 --> 00:12:01.040
These were patients who were already
treated for invasive prostate cancer

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:04.560
by either radical prostatectomy
or radiation therapy,

00:12:04.560 --> 00:12:09.040
yet had rising PSA levels suggesting
the treatment didn’t work,

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.000
and the cancer
was on the move.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:17.120
In those with a cancer recurrence,
PSA levels typically rise exponentially,

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:22.080
reflecting the gradual, inexorable
growth of the cancer in the body.

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:25.280
The rate of this PSA rise
is the single best predictor

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:31.360
of the development of overt metastatic
disease, as well as of overall survival.

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:34.240
The next step would be what’s
called hormonal therapy,

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:39.360
which is chemical or surgical castration,
which has a list of side effects,

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:43.520
including loss of libido and sexual
function and strength and vitality.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:47.200
Therefore, we try to hold off
on that for as long as possible;

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:51.760
so, if we’re just waiting, we
might as well give diet a try.

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:55.920
So, they were taught to increase intake
of whole grains, vegetables, fruit, and beans

00:12:55.920 --> 00:12:59.520
and to decrease meat,
dairy, and refined carbs.

00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:04.400
Of all possible lifestyle interventions,
why a whole food plant-based diet?

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:06.960
Well, if you look around the
world, there are

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:10.080
huge differences in
prostate cancer rates,

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:16.160
with “We’re #1 USA, USA”
rates up to a hundred times

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:19.760
higher than some places
in Asia, for example.

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:24.480
And, it’s not just genetic; within one
generation of coming to the U.S.,

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:30.160
cancer rates shoot up, and the grandkids
end up with the same top-of-the-pile rates.

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:33.040
A whole range of lifestyle
factors have been looked at,

00:13:33.040 --> 00:13:36.560
but diet appears to have
the greatest influence.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:40.560
Specifically, consumption of meat
and dairy appears to increase risk,

00:13:40.560 --> 00:13:43.920
and consumption of plant foods
appears to decrease risk;

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:46.400
hence, the plant-based diet.

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:51.280
A possible mechanism is arachidonic
acid, an inflammatory compound

00:13:51.280 --> 00:13:56.800
which we make from omega-6 rich oils like
corn, sunflower, safflower, and cottonseed,

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:01.520
and also comes preformed
in animal-based foods,

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:06.480
particularly in chicken in the
American diet, and also eggs.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:10.080
And, in a petri dish, at least,
arachidonic acid appears to stimulate

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:13.600
prostate cancer cell
growth as much 200%.

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:17.680
But, ask men to remove processed
and animal foods from their diet

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:21.190
for six months and
what happens?

00:14:21.304 --> 00:14:25.520
9 of the 10 patients showed an
apparent slowing of cancer growth,

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:30.080
and 4 of the 9 an apparent
reversal in cancer growth.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:33.440
The average doubling time, an
estimate of how long it takes

00:14:33.440 --> 00:14:35.760
for their cancer
to double in size,

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:40.560
slowed from doubling every year
to closer to every 10 years.

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:44.320
There’s been other studies using various
diets and nutritional interventions,

00:14:44.320 --> 00:14:49.680
like vitamin supplements, but none
have worked as well as this one.

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:53.120
And their compliance wasn’t
even all that great.

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:55.840
They did good about boosting
their whole grain consumption,

00:14:55.840 --> 00:15:00.080
especially in those first three
months, but then backslid a bit.

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:02.960
They did eat more vegetables,
including a serving

00:15:02.960 --> 00:15:06.240
of greens and an extra serving
of fruit, at least early on,

00:15:06.240 --> 00:15:11.440
and at least ate one whole serving
of legumes a day, when they started.

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:15.840
So, the researchers did observe some dietary
recidivism by the end of the study.

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:21.520
The patients started out stronger, but then
started to slide back into old habits.

00:15:21.520 --> 00:15:24.560
So, the researchers checked
to see if maybe they were better

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:29.040
able to beat off the disease
during that earlier period,

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:34.880
and indeed, at the end of three months,
on average, there was PSA reversal.

00:15:34.880 --> 00:15:40.320
So, changes in the rate of PSA rise were like
opposite that of whole plant food intake,

00:15:40.320 --> 00:15:45.360
raising the provocative possibility that
PSA may have been tracking those changes

00:15:45.360 --> 00:15:50.320
suggesting that the adoption of a plant-based
diet may have therapeutic potential

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:54.160
in the management of
recurrent prostate cancer.

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:59.360
Their findings suggest that without
further surgery, radiation, or chemo,

00:15:59.360 --> 00:16:03.760
disease progression can be
slowed or even reversed,

00:16:03.760 --> 00:16:06.240
despite the “prevailing
scientific consensus

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:09.520
that cancer progression
is largely irreversible.”

00:16:09.520 --> 00:16:12.800
They're not refuting the
benefits of standard therapies

00:16:12.800 --> 00:16:16.080
and not guaranteeing that a plant-based
diet and stress reduction

00:16:16.080 --> 00:16:17.840
will always
induce remission.

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:21.200
But, the results do contribute to
this growing medical literature

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:26.640
that in at least some circumstances,
cancer may be partly reversible.

00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:32.560
Just by modifying dietary and lifestyle factors,
men may be able to prevent disease spread,

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:36.800
all without getting their
testicles chopped off.

